@page "/privacy"
@attribute [Authorize(Policy = "RequireSignedIn")]
@layout MainLayout

<PageTitle>Privacy information – Varangians</PageTitle>

<PageHeader Title="Privacy information" />

<section class="prose">
    <p><strong>Last updated: 30 October 2026</strong></p>

    <p>Shrewsbury Orthodox Church operates a chapter of Varangians. Shrewsbury Orthodox Church is a charitable incorporated organisation (charity number 1166735) and is responsible for the personal information described here.</p>

    <h2>Who to contact</h2>
    <p>For questions about your information or to exercise your data protection rights, contact Shrewsbury Orthodox Church at <a href="mailto:info@shrewsburyorthodox.com">info@shrewsburyorthodox.com</a>, or write to Greek Orthodox Church, Dove Close, Shrewsbury, SY2 6FB. The church has not appointed a Data Protection Officer.</p>

    <h2>What information we use and why</h2>
    <ul>
        <li><strong>Accounts:</strong> name, email address, password security information, roles and chapter membership. We use these to create and secure accounts, confirm email addresses, provide appropriate access and contact people about the service. A new account has no access until an administrator assigns a role. Some staff roles require two-factor authentication before app access is available.</li>
        <li><strong>Children and families:</strong> a child's name, date of birth, chapter, enrolment and leaving dates, parent or guardian relationship and contact details. Parent applications also include a phone number and postal address. We use these to assess applications, enrol children, communicate with families and run each chapter.</li>
        <li><strong>Programme records:</strong> lesson progress, rank, attendance, badges and certificates. We use these to support children's participation and record their progress.</li>
        <li><strong>Consent and safety:</strong> parent choices about photographs and media, transport, off-site activities and data sharing; consent status and expiry; emergency contact details; and any emergency medical, dietary or special educational needs information provided. We use these to plan activities, respond to emergencies and safeguard children. The app records photo and media choices; it does not store photographs.</li>
        <li><strong>Staff and volunteers:</strong> names, email addresses, chapter roles and staff status. Authorised administrators and safeguarding officers record DBS check status, check and expiry dates and certificate number. The app does not store a copy of a DBS certificate.</li>
        <li><strong>Security and service records:</strong> records of who accessed sensitive safeguarding information and when, plus technical logs such as IP address, browser details and requests made to the site. We use these to protect accounts and information, investigate incidents and keep the service working.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>We receive account details from the person registering, child and family details from parents or guardians and authorised staff, and programme records from authorised staff or from activity within the app. An authorised safeguarding officer or administrator records DBS details after checking the certificate.</p>

    <h2>Who can see information</h2>
    <p>Access is limited by role and chapter. Parents and guardians can see information about children linked to them. Students can see their own programme information. Teachers can see the programme information needed to run their chapter and, when they have set up two-factor authentication, can read children's emergency, medical, dietary and special educational needs details. Chapter administrators and safeguarding officers can review applications and access the sensitive information needed for their responsibilities; two-factor authentication is required. DBS records are restricted to administrators and safeguarding officers. Access to sensitive information is recorded.</p>
    <p>Information is processed for Shrewsbury Orthodox Church by DigitalOcean (the application and database hosted in London), Cloudflare (off-site backup storage in the European Union) and Microsoft (email sent through the church's Microsoft 365 service). Hostinger provides domain name services. These providers process information under their service and data-protection terms. We do not sell personal information or use it for advertising.</p>

    <h2>Our reasons for using information</h2>
    <p>For ordinary account, family, programme, staff and security records, we rely on our legitimate interests in operating Varangians chapters, keeping children safe and administering the programme. We have considered the effect on individuals and limit access and use to those purposes. We rely on consent for activities where a parent is asked to choose, such as photographs and media, transport, off-site trips and data sharing. You can change or withdraw those choices by contacting your chapter administrator; this does not affect earlier use made while consent was in place.</p>
    <p>Health and other sensitive information is used only where needed for the care and safety of members of the church's religious community, under the applicable data-protection conditions for not-for-profit bodies with a religious aim. DBS information is used for safeguarding and volunteer checks under the applicable conditions for not-for-profit bodies and safeguarding children. We do not publish these details or include them in ordinary email notifications.</p>

    <h2>How long we keep information</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Child records, including consent and health-related details, are kept while a child takes part and for up to three years after they leave or graduate. They are then flagged for an administrator to delete.</li>
        <li>Applications, including unsuccessful applications, are kept for up to one year after a decision and then flagged for deletion.</li>
        <li>DBS records are kept until six years after the check expires or the staff member leaves, whichever comes first, and are then flagged for deletion.</li>
        <li>Records of access to sensitive information are kept for six years. Technical server logs are kept for up to 90 days.</li>
        <li>Account information is kept while the account is needed to provide access or maintain programme records. Contact the church if you believe an account is no longer needed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Deletion from the live system is carried out by an administrator after the record becomes due. Encrypted off-site database backups are kept for up to 35 days, so a deleted record may remain in a backup until that backup expires. Off-site copies of stored files also expire after 35 days; files still needed by the service are copied again during the next nightly backup. Backups are used only for recovery.</p>

    <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
    <p>You can ask for a copy of your personal information, ask us to correct it, or ask us to erase it, restrict its use or provide it in a portable form where the law gives you that right. You can object to use based on our legitimate interests; we will stop unless we have compelling grounds to continue or need the information for legal claims. Some rights depend on the reason we use the information and may be limited where safeguarding or another legal obligation applies. Contact the church using the details above.</p>
    <p><strong>You can withdraw consent at any time</strong> for an activity choice, by contacting your chapter administrator. This will not affect processing that took place before you withdrew it.</p>
    <p>You can also complain to the Information Commissioner's Office (ICO) at <a href="https://ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint/">ico.org.uk/make-a-complaint</a> or call 0303 123 1113.</p>

    <h2>Children and automated decisions</h2>
    <p>We use children's information to run the programme and keep them safe. We explain collection on the relevant forms, and parents or guardians provide application and consent information. Children can speak with their parent or guardian or a trusted chapter adult if they have questions. We do not make decisions about people solely by automated processing where those decisions have a legal or similarly significant effect. Some programme rules are applied automatically to records, but staff remain responsible for decisions about membership, safeguarding and applications.</p>
</section>
